Since you used GNOME to take the screenshot, it was automatically saved to a dedicated system folder rather than your current terminal directory.
GNOME automatically names screenshots using the format Screenshot from YYYY-MM-DD HH-MM-SS.png.
## 1. View the most recent screenshot instantly
You can open the absolute newest file in your GNOME screenshot folder by running this single command in your terminal:

xdg-open "$(ls -td ~/Pictures/Screenshots/* | head -n 1)"

## 2. Find the file name manually
If you want to see the exact file name first, list the contents of your GNOME screenshot directory sorted by time (newest at the bottom):

ls -lrt ~/Pictures/Screenshots/

Once you see the name, you can open it with xdg-open ~/Pictures/Screenshots/"File Name.png".
## 3. If you used an older GNOME version
Older versions of GNOME saved screenshots directly to the main Pictures folder instead of a subfolder. If the commands above return an error, check there:

ls -lrt ~/Pictures/

